# Modal and Drawer Contract

## Choose the overlay deliberately

- Use a modal for a focused task, consequential confirmation, or information that must interrupt background interaction.
- Use a drawer or inspector for contextual detail that benefits from keeping the underlying collection visible.
- Use a dedicated route when the task is large, deeply navigable, or must be linkable and resumable.
- Do not use an overlay merely to avoid designing a page.


- A deliberately long creation workflow belongs in a drawer, staged page, or dedicated route rather than a scrolling creation modal. Incidental overflow inside an otherwise bounded modal remains acceptable.

## Modal requirements

- accessible name and correct dialog semantics;
- background inert while open;
- intentional initial focus;
- Tab and Shift+Tab contained;
- Escape closes unless a documented critical workflow forbids it;
- visible close or cancel control;
- focus returns to the invoker or a documented logical destination;
- viewport-relative maximum height;
- exactly one intended vertical scroll owner for long content;
- title/context and critical actions remain reachable while the body scrolls;
- background page does not scroll;
- narrow and full-width states remain usable.

For dense schedule lists, ten or more items must be exercised. The shell remains bounded while the list body scrolls; pagination is used only if item count, performance, or task model justifies it.

## Drawer requirements

- clear relationship to the selected item;
- close behavior and focus return;
- no accidental content coverage at supported widths;
- declared modal or non-modal interaction model;
- list selection and details remain synchronized;
- narrow widths transform deliberately, often into a full-screen detail route or sheet.

## Required states

open, long/dense content, narrow viewport, validation error, loading, empty, success, cancel, Escape close, outside interaction according to contract, and nested confirmation only when unavoidable.

## Mechanical evidence

dialog/drawer role and name, inertness, initial focus, tab containment, Escape, close control, focus return, page/body scroll lock, shell and body bounding boxes, scroll-owner count, footer visibility, clipping, and background interaction checks.

